Jason, you are not done yet. ggplot2_0.8.3.tar.gz is a source package,
ready for distribution. In order to use it on WIndows, you will need
to get it converted to a binary package, using winbuilder.

You need to do the following.
1) Edit the ggplot2/DESCRIPTION file in order to change the maintainer
email from Hadleys to your own.
2) Issue R CMD build and create a file ggplot2_0.8.3.tar.gz (but note
that this one has a changed email address)
3) Run
   R CMD check ggplot_0.8.3.tar.gz
to make sure that 2) was succesful.
3) submit ggplot2_0.8.3.tar.gz to "winbuilder" using the instructions on
        http://129.217.207.166/index.htm
(starting from bullet point 3) which will return you a file called
ggplot2_0.8.3.zip

That final file ggplot2_0.8.3.zip is ready for installation on Windows.

Kasper

On Mar 28, 2009, at 20:44 , Jason Rupert wrote:

>
>
> Oh...I think I may finally have it.
>
> Here is the process I came up with for downloading and producing a
> package of the development version for ggplot2:
> (1) Using your favorite method, download latest development version
> of ggplot2 from github. For example, navigate to
> http://github.com/hadley/ggplot2/tree/master,
> select download, and choose either zip or tar.gz.
>
> (2) Once the hadley-ggplot2-xxx.zip or hadley-ggplot2-xxx.tar.gz is
> downloaded, uncompress, and rename the uncompressed folder to just
> ggplot2.  For example,
> .../Documents/hadley-ggplot2-
> c18612033a01175fe1c4396b9d51ada6131e81e8.zip
> after uncompressing it becomes
> .../Documents/hadley-ggplot2-c18612033a01175fe1c4396b9d51ada6131e81e8
> and then renaming it becomes
> .../Documents/ggplot2
>
> (3) Now, open the terminal and navigate to the folder where the
> uncompressed and renamed folder is stored, i.e. step accomplished in
> (2).
> For example,
> $ pwd
> .../Documents
> $ ls
> ggplot2
> ...
> $ R CMD build ggplot2
>
>
> (4) Once in the terminal Window, type "R CMD build ggplot2" to build
> the ggplot2_0.8.3.tar.gz package, which can be used on a Windows
> machine via install.packages("ggplot2").
>
> The results should be similar to the following:
> $ R CMD build ggplot2
> * checking for file 'ggplot2/DESCRIPTION' ... OK
> * preparing 'ggplot2':
> * checking DESCRIPTION meta-information ... OK
> * removing junk files
> * checking for LF line-endings in source and make files
> * checking for empty or unneeded directories
> * building 'ggplot2_0.8.3.tar.gz'
>
>
> I haven't had a chance to test out the resulting
> ggplot2_0.8.3.tar.gz on a Windows machine, but should be able to
> very soon be able to check that out.
>
> Thank you again for all the help getting this far.  I am really
> looking forward to using some of the development additions Hadley
> has been working on.
>
> Take care.
>
>
