The more I think about this, i can see an opportunity to design a new
type of geom and scales. The TeachingDemos package provides the
concept of an arbitrary polygon to be used as a symbol in a plot,

 my.symbols( 1:10, runif(10), ms.male, add=FALSE, xlab='x',
ylab='y', inches=0.3, col=c('blue','green'), xlim=c(0,11), ylim=c(-0.1,1.1))
my.symbols( (1:10)+0.5, runif(10), ms.female, add=TRUE, inches=0.3,
col=c('red','green') )

One could think of a situation where the symbol shape is varied
according to a factor or continuous variable (say, small ellipses of
varying aspect ratio), not only the size of it as currently done in
ggplot2. Similarly, my initial problem would simply translate in the
filling colour of the symbols mapped to a factor.

Would it be easy to create a geom_symbol() similar to geom_point() but
for which the point is an arbitrary grob ?

Best wishes,

baptiste

